{
  "abstract": "We study a common regular fifth of two quadruples covering an explicitly parametrized, fully rooted Piezas sextuple surface. On its finite admissible chart, this would give a rational Diophantine septuple. The common-fifth equation splits into several components. We eliminate all linear components by complete rational-point classifications on two genus-three curves, with nine and eleven points respectively. The certificates combine unconditional Jacobian rank bounds, Coleman integration, exact residue-disc enumeration and a hyperelliptic-involution argument excluding unoccupied Weierstrass discs. A rank-zero elliptic quotient eliminates a further quadratic component. A direct symmetric-polynomial audit includes every finite product-one fifth, avoiding the degree-drop omission in the usual extension formula. Up to a parameter reflection, the fixed pairing therefore reduces to one irreducible genus-twenty curve. An explicit square identity removes its apparent remaining source-root requirement, and an exact function-field decoder verifies all twenty-one roots and generic distinctness of the seven entries. We additionally exclude one component of an alternative pairing using a rank-one genus-two curve. The supplement contains complete arithmetic inputs, raw CAS certificates, independent portable checks, and a standalone decoder calibrated at a known sextuple. No admissible rational point on the genus-twenty curve is supplied. The source is a two-parameter subfamily of classical Piezas sextuples; unrestricted extensions, other residual components and compactification limits remain open.",
